meta {
  name: 02 Learn
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/reading/exercise?modelltest=1
  body: none
  auth: none
}

params:query {
  modelltest: 1
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{studentAccessToken}}
}

docs {
  The request the whole phase is about. Reading is one of seven learning
  controllers; all of them behave identically here, so if you want to check
  another, swap the URL for any of:

  - /api/reading/exercise
  - /api/listening/teils
  - /api/modelltests
  - /api/speaking/teils
  - /api/sprachbausteine/exercise
  - /api/writing/exercises

  ## Entitled: 200

  ## Blocked: 403

  ```json
  {
    "message": "SUBSCRIPTION_INACTIVE",
    "subscriptionStatus": "BLOCKED"
  }
  ```

  403, not 401 — deliberately. The student is perfectly authenticated; telling
  them to log in again would send them round a loop that cannot help them. And
  `subscriptionStatus` rides along so the app can show the offer to carry on
  rather than a dead error.

  ## The thing to actually verify

  Do NOT log in again after blocking the center. Use the SAME
  {{studentAccessToken}} you already had.

  It still fails. That is the point: the check runs on every request, not at
  login, so a student blocked on Monday does not keep working until their token
  happens to expire on Friday.

  Then restore, and re-run with that same untouched token. It works again
  immediately.

  ## Other checks

  - no Authorization header -> 401, not 403. Identity is a different question
    from entitlement, and they answer separately.
  - a CENTER access token -> 401. The student verifier refuses center tokens.
  - a student with no center -> 200 always. Nobody's subscription governs them,
    which is what keeps every pre-center user working the day this ships.
}
